==================== thread idx 6 | threadId: PRRT_kwDORYAuYs6qtjZO | rootCommentId: 4228369444
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the AC3 gate can strand approved PRs with unresolved suggestion threads on conversation-gated repos**

`armReworkDecision` withholds the rework arm whenever the effective verdict is APPROVE with `blocking == 0`:

```js
if (rec.verdict === 'APPROVE') {
    var blocking = Number(rec.blocking || 0) || 0;
    if (blocking > 0) {
        return { arm: true, reason: 'blocking-threads', effective: effective };
    }
    return { arm: false, reason: 'approve-verdict', effective: effective };
}
```

The gate is correct as the sticky-approval rule (AC3), but its census condition is narrower than the merge-stall condition. On repos with "require conversation resolution" (the exact gate gh-683 documented: *"an APPROVE verdict with unresolved threads deadlocks merge without it"*, live fa #1211 14:13), an approve-with-suggestions review (`allowApproveWithSuggestions: true` — the factory's documented terminal state) posts N suggestion threads and stamps `APPROVE {blocking: 0, suggestions: N}`. Then:

- `rework-unresolved-threads` matches (`threadsResolved: false`) but the gate withholds the arm;
- `merge-validated` requires `mergeState: CLEAN` — the conversation gate keeps it `BLOCKED`;
- no other leg resolves threads: the rework leg owns them by design, and every re-review armer excludes `pr_approved` (sticky approval = no re-review).

Net: the PR strands green + approved with open threads until a human resolves one — the gh-683 blind-spot shape re-entering through the one rule built to close it.

Suggested remedies (any one): (a) qualify the gate with the record census — arm when `blocking == 0 && suggestions > 0` on a head with unresolved threads, or (b) keep the withhold but have the tick resolve the stale suggestion threads it owns (a "resolve, don't rework" action), or (c) at minimum document the caveat in the rule description and name the conversation-gate risk in the withhold log line. The blocking-threads exception shows the census is already trusted — this just extends it one column.

==================== thread idx 7 | threadId: PRRT_kwDORYAuYs6qtjcP | rootCommentId: 4228369785
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: verdict records are unauthenticated — any PR participant can forge one and steer the machine loop**

`extractVerdictRecord` accepts the marker from ANY comment, regardless of author:

```js
var start = text.indexOf(MARKER_PREFIX);
...
try { parsed = JSON.parse(raw); } catch (e) { return null; }
if (!parsed || !parsed.head || !parsed.verdict) return null;
```

The comment payload carries `author`/`created_at` (the doc even says "accepted but not required"), but the reader never checks it. The marker format is public (this repo), so anyone with comment access to a machine-loop PR can post a newer record than the legs':

- a forged `APPROVE` (newest timestamp) makes `armReworkDecision` withhold rework arms and turns the `latestVerdict: [APPROVE]` reconcile shapes into label strippers — a legitimate red-free `agent:rework` arm placed by the RC verdict comes off on the next tick;
- a forged `APPROVE` defeats the `notLatestVerdict` lanes, un-blocking `pr_approved`-gated validation/merge paths the real review had locked.

The blast radius is bounded — the machine grants no label from records (only `postPRReviewComments` adds `pr_approved`), `merge-validated` still needs machine-owned `ai_validating` + green + CLEAN, and reconciliation only removes labels — so this is automation sabotage / loop stalling, not a merge bypass. But it is a new unauthenticated input channel into state that previously only the machine could write.

Suggested hardening (small): trust only records authored by the machine identity — `parseVerdictRecords`/`extractVerdictRecord` already receives the payload; filter on `item.user.login === machineAuthor` (or the reviewing identity) in `smProvider.verdictRecords`/`readVerdictRecords` before parsing, fail-open to `[]` like the other paths. Add a forge-replay test (non-machine-author record → ignored) alongside the AC4 free-form one.

==================== thread idx 8 | threadId: PRRT_kwDORYAuYs6qtjfv | rootCommentId: 4228370138
💡 **SUGGESTION (restate from round 2, still open by choice): the reconcile strip is census-blind — APPROVE + blocking>0 arms yo-yo on green heads**

`reconcileDecision` exempts CI-driven arms only via `state.checksRed`:

```js
if (loser === LABEL_REWORK && st.checksRed) return null;
```

One residual interplay on **green** heads: `rework-unresolved-threads` legitimately arms the PR carrier when the APPROVE record's census reports `blocking > 0` (AC3's blocking-threads exception — the gate allows it), but `reconcile-rework-vs-approval` (green head → `checksRed` false) still strips that arm. It self-heals — the arm rule re-adds after reconcile in rule order and the gate allows on `blocking > 0` — but each cycle costs one tick of dispatch delay plus a ⚖️ comment claiming "suggestions do not justify a rework arm" while the record itself says the findings were BLOCKING.

The record's own census is the evidence the arm was granted on; treating it like `checksRed` is a one-line change:

```js
if (loser === LABEL_REWORK &&
    (st.checksRed || (Number(effective.record.blocking || 0) || 0) > 0)) return null;
```

Plus a replay test: APPROVE record (blocking=2) + unresolved threads + PR-carrier arm on a green head ⇒ arm survives, no comment. Non-blocking — the loop converges today; this only removes a tick of churn and a misleading comment.

